<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#palayakkarar">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palayakkarar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Poligars, Poligar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palayakkarar, known in English as Poligars and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> as Palegars, were military chiefs of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> who held territories called palayams under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and its successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> states in return for military service and tribute. Many, such as the Nayakas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a>, became virtually independent rulers in the 17th and 18th centuries. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> subdued the last of them in the Poligar Wars of 1799–1805.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Palaiyakkarar means ruler of a citadel in Tamil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Telugu and Malayalam. A palayam, a fortified town or military camp, was granted for valuable military service to a holder who ruled it as a feudatory of a greater sovereign. The system was already in use under Pratapa Rudhra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> in the Kakatiya kingdom, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> it was put in place by Viswanatha Nayak when he became Nayak ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1529, with the support of his minister Ariyanatha Mudaliar. Tradition held that there were 72 Palayakkarars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Palayakkarars administered their territories from fortified centres. They collected taxes, kept order, ran the local judiciary and maintained a battalion of troops for the king, retaining a quarter of the revenue and sending the rest to the royal treasury. They also founded villages, built dams and tanks, and constructed temples. Most of those who held power in the late 17th and 18th centuries came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallar">Kallar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> and Vatuka communities: Maravar in western Tirunelveli and Ramanathapuram, Kallar around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, Tiruchi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> in eastern Tirunelveli, Dindigal and Coimbatore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Polygar Wars were fought by coalitions of Palaiyakkarars against the British East India Company over the Carnatic. The war with Veerapandiya Kattabomman is often called the First Polygar War, and the Second, from 1800 to 1805, was fought by a larger coalition across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> under leaders including Dheeran Chinnamalai and Maruthu Pandiyar. The Polygars often had artillery and stubbornly resisted attacks on their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a>, and British columns were harassed as they cut through dense jungle. A last war was fought in 1847 by Uyyalawada Narasimha Reddy at Kovelakuntla.</p><p>After the defeat of the rebels some were executed and others banished to the Andaman Islands. Some who submitted worked as dubashes or received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> status, which gave only tax-collecting rights and left them disarmed. The class lost authority after the British Permanent Settlement of the early 19th century, though its influence lasted until the land reforms of 1947 to 1950.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polygar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nayakas, also called <b>Palegars</b>, ruled a dry upland territory of central Karnataka through warrior clans and revenue from farming and herding.</blockquote><a href="/state/chitradurga-nayakas">Chitradurga Nayakas</a></li><li><blockquote>He organised the region into some seventy-two palayams held by military chiefs, the <b>Palaiyakkarars</b>, and his successors ruled the southern Tamil country, becoming independent after Vijayanagara's decline.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
